<section>
  <div class="section-head">
    <div class="kicker">Season Four</div>
    <h2>The Clues &amp; The Reveals</h2>
  </div>


  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S4E26 → S4E28 · Clues given December 9 · Revealed December 21, 2025</div>
    <h3>Mesa, Arizona</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>The city is named for a geological formation</li>
      <li>Founded in the 1870s</li>
      <li>Canals have been an important part of this city since prior to European visitation</li>
      <li>Some indigenous plants are very well protected — huge legal protections</li>
      <li>The city hosts some big-league entertainment, if the season is right</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">Mesa, AZ</span></div>
    <p class="winners">🏆 Winner: Keith. Alex earned half points for landing just 20 miles away.
    Hohokam canals over a thousand years old, fiercely protected saguaros, and Cactus League
    spring training. For our fans in Mesa: don't cut down any cactus.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S4E25 → S4E26 · Clues given November 23 · Revealed December 9, 2025</div>
    <h3>Tucker, Georgia</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>Founded in the late 1800s, yet one of the state's newest cities</li>
      <li>The high school stadium and campus is one of the stars of the community</li>
      <li>The original area of town was part of a military district</li>
      <li>An unlucky number of cemeteries sit within a few miles of the city limits</li>
      <li>After World War II, the town transitioned from agriculture to industry and retail</li>
      <li>Michael's home state's namesake is represented somehow by this town</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">Tucker, GA</span></div>
    <p class="winners">Military District 572 (later DeKalb County), 13 cemeteries within four miles,
    and Steve Walsh — vocalist of the band Kansas — calls it home. Armando's guess of Bentonville,
    Arkansas earned points for cycling taste, but not for accuracy.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S4E24 → S4E25 · Clues given November 9 · Revealed November 23, 2025</div>
    <h3>Waltham, Massachusetts</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>This community was named for a town in England</li>
      <li>It was first settled as part of another town</li>
      <li>Incorporated in the 1700s</li>
      <li>Responsible for greatly increasing industrial productivity across the United States</li>
      <li>Tracking time was very important to this city's development and history</li>
      <li>It maintains neighborhood names that reflect its industrial past</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">Waltham, MA — "Watch City"</span></div>
    <p class="winners">🏆 Winner: Matt Robinson. Armando went Newark, Alex went Lancaster PA.
    Born from Watertown in 1738, birthplace of integrated manufacturing, home of the Bleachery
    and the Chemistry — and the Waltham Watch Company, which made 35 million watches.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S4E23 → S4E24 · Clues posted online after October 26 · Revealed November 9, 2025</div>
    <h3>Fort Payne, Alabama</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>Started as a fort built in the early 1800s to intern the native Cherokee population</li>
      <li>Incorporated in the late 1800s, with growth spurred by a rail line</li>
      <li>Coal and iron finds in the 1880s proved smaller than hoped, causing decline</li>
      <li>Cotton processing boomed</li>
      <li>An annual festival celebrates its world standing in hosiery (sock capital of the world)</li>
      <li>"Truly a Dixieland delight" (the band Alabama is from there)</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">Fort Payne, AL</span></div>
    <p class="winners">🏆 The first-ever FOUR winners on one mystery: Wade Paul, Alex Bell, Matt Robinson
    (who offered to eat his socks if wrong — he owns 300+ pairs), and Armando.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S4E22 → S4E23 · Clues given October 12 · Revealed October 26, 2025</div>
    <h3>Fort Wayne, Indiana</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>Area controlled by several countries (French, British, US), with many battles between native tribes and the US</li>
      <li>Founded in the late 1700s (1794), named after founding father Anthony Wayne</li>
      <li>An invention that changed what we eat forever (the refrigerator)</li>
      <li>An early economy of industry and railroad jobs at a strategic confluence of three rivers</li>
      <li>Influenced "a place where everybody knows your name" (Cheers' Shelley Long, born there)</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">Fort Wayne, IN</span></div>
    <p class="winners">No correct guesses — Adam worked through Pittsburgh and Boston before losing.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S4E21 → S4E22 · Clues given October 2 · Revealed October 12, 2025</div>
    <h3>Prescott, Arizona</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>A stereotypical Wild West town, still big with cowboys (the world's oldest rodeo, continuous since 1888)</li>
      <li>Spanish visited in 1583 seeking gold; gold was found in 1863</li>
      <li>Territorial capital of Arizona — twice (1867 and 1877)</li>
      <li>A Doc Holliday connection (Big Nose Kate was from Prescott)</li>
      <li>A downtown that would make Adam smile (Whiskey Row)</li>
      <li>Presidential ambitions sparked there (Barry Goldwater launched his 1964 campaign on the courthouse steps)</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">Prescott, AZ</span></div>
    <p class="winners">New guesser Terry Donaldson from Largo, FL went Cimarron, NM — wrong, but "right part
    of the country" (Michael spent five summers at nearby Philmont Scout Ranch).</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S4E20 → S4E21 · Clues given September 14 · Revealed October 2, 2025</div>
    <h3>Bay City, Michigan</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>Settled 1831; first named after a river (Lower Saginaw)</li>
      <li>Grew through lumbering, milling, and shipbuilding (154 WWII ships built, only three lost)</li>
      <li>1980s fashion — it "helped us understand Vogue" (Madonna was born there)</li>
      <li>A tall ships festival</li>
      <li>The lumber boom named a section of town (Hell's Half Mile)</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">Bay City, MI</span></div>
    <p class="winners">Michael's home-state pick for Adam. Wrong guess: David Stroud — Newport News, VA.
    The Bay City Rollers, it turns out, are from Edinburgh.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S4E19 → S4E20 · Clues given August 31 · Revealed September 14, 2025</div>
    <h3>Bismarck, North Dakota</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>Originally named Edwinton, renamed for German chancellor Otto von Bismarck</li>
      <li>A zoo that began as a rescue in 1961 — one baby deer, now 600+ animals</li>
      <li>"Surprisingly bright" (200+ days of sun a year)</li>
      <li>Lewis and Clark passed through</li>
      <li>Helped end WWII (a hub of uranium exploration)</li>
      <li>An Art Deco record-holder (the State Capitol — 21 stories, tallest in ND)</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">Bismarck, ND</span></div>
    <p class="winners">Wrong guess: Tony from Caldwell, ID — Manhattan, KS (about an hour from Michael's house).</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S4E18 → S4E19 · Clues given August 17 · Revealed August 31, 2025</div>
    <h3>Johnson City, Tennessee</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>Founded 1856 as a railroad station by Henry Johnson; renamed Haynesville during the Civil War, then changed back</li>
      <li>Organized crime and bootlegging (a rumored Al Capone distribution center)</li>
      <li>Old-time music</li>
      <li>A soft drink (Mountain Dew, created there in the 1940s)</li>
      <li>"A trucker heading west was on his way to this town" (Wagon Wheel)</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">Johnson City, TN</span></div>
    <p class="winners">🏆 Winner: Wade Paul, with a mic-drop clue-by-clue answer.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S4E17 → S4E18 · Clues given August 3 · Revealed August 17, 2025 — episode 75</div>
    <h3>Atlanta, Illinois</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>Platted in the 1830s; two railroads intersect the town</li>
      <li>Tried to rename itself twice — paperwork misspelled "Atalanta" with one A too many</li>
      <li>Named for a community in Georgia; sits on historic Route 66</li>
      <li>"Would make any lumberjack proud — or Oscar Mayer" (the Bunyon Giant, a 19-foot Paul Bunyan holding a giant hot dog)</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">Atlanta, IL</span></div>
    <p class="winners">Partial credit: Matt Robinson — Vienna, IL. Right state, wrong town, silent S intentionally pronounced.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S4E16 → S4E17 · Clues given July 20 · Revealed August 3, 2025</div>
    <h3>New Hope, Pennsylvania</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>Sits on the former main highway between two major cities (Old York Road, Philadelphia–New York)</li>
      <li>"George dreamed of a great victory there" (Washington, the night before crossing the Delaware)</li>
      <li>"Monopoly has nothing on this community's railroad" (taken over by the Reading Railroad in 1891)</li>
      <li>Shelled by the British — ordnance reportedly still lodged in roof beams</li>
      <li>Founded in the 1830s and named after a local ferry</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">New Hope, PA</span></div>
    <p class="winners">Wrong guess: Matthew from Ohio (the friend who rescued their bike boxes at their
    first Big BRAG) — Wheeling, WV.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S4E15 → S4E16 · Clues given July 6 · Revealed July 20, 2025</div>
    <h3>Grafton, Wisconsin</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>A German-and-Irish mill town on the Milwaukee River</li>
      <li>Early blues and jazz success (Paramount Records' studio and HQ from 1925)</li>
      <li>Sits on a historic "trail" (a northern point on the Mississippi Blues Trail)</li>
      <li>The Secret Service clue (Charles Guiteau, President Garfield's assassin, grew up nearby)</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">Grafton, WI</span></div>
    <p class="winners">Wrong guess: Tom from Utah — Sioux Falls, SD.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S4E15 reveal · Clues posted during the June BRAG break · Revealed July 6, 2025</div>
    <h3>Rome, Italy</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>Your cat would love this city</li>
      <li>History everywhere — even in a well-known fast food restaurant</li>
      <li>The city owes a great deal to a particular church</li>
      <li>One of its fountains could fund a stylish retirement (the Trevi)</li>
      <li>A distinct article of clothing instantly associated with it (the toga)</li>
      <li>An association with that year's Big BRAG (the ride spent time in Rome, GA)</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">Rome, Italy</span></div>
    <p class="winners">An international curveball. Wrong guesses: Trip Barker — Hartwell, GA; Adam —
    Toccoa, GA. Adam demanded the challenge flag.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S4E11 → S4E12 · Clues given May 11 · Revealed May 25, 2025</div>
    <h3>Huntsville, Alabama</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>Started in a territory named for a different state (Mississippi Territory)</li>
      <li>Home to nine Civil War generals — five Confederate, four Union</li>
      <li>Name changed from the British "Twickenham" amid War of 1812 tensions</li>
      <li>A WWII military presence (Redstone Arsenal — chemical weapons, then rockets)</li>
      <li>Could have inspired a well-known Elton John song (Rocket Man)</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">Huntsville, AL</span></div>
    <p class="winners">🏆 ADAM'S FIRST-EVER WIN — "after like 60 of these" — called in two minutes after
    recording; plus Wade Paul and Jeff (whose wife figured it out). Alex Bell's Lexington, VA: a
    beautifully reasoned wrong answer.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S4E10 → S4E11 · Clues given April 27 · Revealed May 11, 2025</div>
    <h3>Naples, Florida</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>Founded in the 1880s by a former Confederate general and Kentucky US Senator</li>
      <li>Likened to Italy early on — hence Naples</li>
      <li>Golf capital of the world (more holes per capita than anywhere in the US)</li>
      <li>A pickleball championship every year since 2016</li>
      <li>No fountain of youth, but perhaps the seven cities of gold (one of the wealthiest US cities)</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">Naples, FL</span></div>
    <p class="winners">🏆 Winner: Armando ("I don't get to do this very often" — Michael). Wrong: Matt
    Robinson — Yuma; Melanie Erickson and Adam — The Villages; Jeff — Venice, FL.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S4E9 → S4E10 · Clues given April 13 · Revealed April 27, 2025</div>
    <h3>Broken Arrow, Oklahoma</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>Named for a community in Alabama — honoring the Creek people expelled from Alabama</li>
      <li>Railroad arrived 1902; town platted 1903</li>
      <li>Agriculture, then coal mining; now a suburb of Tulsa</li>
      <li>Third-largest manufacturing hub in Oklahoma, within the Muscogee Creek Reservation</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">Broken Arrow, OK</span></div>
    <p class="winners">No correct guesses; Adam: St. Louis ("not close"). Dedicated to the listeners who
    had been there since roughly episode two.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S4E8 → S4E9 · Clues given March 30 · Revealed April 13, 2025</div>
    <h3>Hampton, New Hampshire</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>Settled in the early-to-mid 1600s by English settlers; farming and fishing first</li>
      <li>A recreational destination since the mid-1800s (Hampton Beach drew all of New England)</li>
      <li>Railroad arrived 1840; named for a location in England</li>
      <li>One of the oldest towns in its state</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">Hampton, NH</span></div>
    <p class="winners">Wrong guess: Al — Atlantic City, NJ; close regionally. New listeners welcomed that
    episode from the Netherlands, Portugal, Hungary, and Belize.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S4E7 → S4E8 · Clues given March 16 · Revealed March 30, 2025</div>
    <h3>Marmaduke, Arkansas</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>Named for Confederate Maj. Gen. John Sappington Marmaduke, who camped nearby</li>
      <li>Laid out by the railroad in the 1880s; lumber first, then cotton</li>
      <li>Half the town destroyed by a tornado on April 2, 2008</li>
      <li>Felt the 1911 New Madrid Fault earthquake</li>
      <li>Nearly the smallest community highlighted (pop. 1,212 — one of them their listener)</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">Marmaduke, AR</span></div>
    <p class="winners">Very close guess: Johnny — New Madrid/Sikeston, MO, the quake's epicenter.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S4E6 → S4E7 · Clues given March 2 · Revealed March 16, 2025</div>
    <h3>South Jordan, Utah</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>Area first explored by a French-Canadian trapper named Provost</li>
      <li>First settlement created by a religious group (LDS), founded mid-1850s</li>
      <li>Irrigation was the settlers' first big job</li>
      <li>The reason bus drivers stop and open the door at railroad crossings (the Dec 1, 1938 bus-train
      crash — the worst in US history — led to the federal law)</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">South Jordan, UT</span></div>
    <p class="winners">No guesses; Adam's off-record hunch was New Mexico.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S4E5 → S4E6 · Clues given February 16 · Revealed March 2, 2025</div>
    <h3>Millsboro, Delaware</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>English settlers drawn by the timber drive; 15+ grist and saw mills within four miles</li>
      <li>Original name changed before 1810 (Rock Hole Mills → Millsboro)</li>
      <li>Known for extremes — holds BOTH Delaware temperature records (−17°F and 110°F)</li>
      <li>Named a top remote-working spot by PC Magazine in 2021</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">Millsboro, DE</span></div>
    <p class="winners">Wrong guesses: Cricket — "below the Mason-Dixon line" (no city named); Angie Raffey
    via YouTube — Rock Run, MD.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S4E4 → S4E5 · Clues given February 2 · Revealed February 16, 2025</div>
    <h3>Princeton, West Virginia</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>A community in a state it did not start in (Virginia land before West Virginia existed)</li>
      <li>Locals burned their own town rather than let the Union army use it</li>
      <li>Named for a Revolutionary battle (honoring Princeton, NJ)</li>
      <li>Bulls fans can thank this town (Rod Thorn, who drafted Michael Jordan)</li>
      <li>Once had a team that would make you want a bourbon (the Princeton WhistlePigs)</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">Princeton, WV</span></div>
    <p class="winners">No guesses.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S4E3 → S4E4 · Clues given January 19 · Revealed February 2, 2025</div>
    <h3>York, Pennsylvania</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>Brought the luxury of train travel to the road (the Pullman)</li>
      <li>"You could say the US was born here" (an early US capital — the Continental Congress fled there)</li>
      <li>The first successful coal-burning locomotive (1831)</li>
      <li>A candy bears its name (the York Peppermint Pattie)</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">York, PA</span></div>
    <p class="winners">🏆 Winner: Matt Robinson — finally congratulated by his correct name, after
    Michael's running Matt/Mark confusion.</p>
  </div>


